browser-extension/ is the Chrome companion extension of pi-chrome 0.15.51
(https://github.com/tianrendong/pi-chrome, commit 4811ee6, MIT — see LICENSE),
with these changes, and no others:

- Named for Nodecode: manifest name, title and description ("Nodecode Chrome
  Connector"), the poll's client name (the bridge hands commands only to a
  poll carrying it), the "nc" badge, the default tab-group title and the
  console prefix. The fourth version part counts these changes.
- groupTab creates a new group in the tab's own window
  (createProperties.windowId). Upstream's call puts it in the last-focused
  window, which moved the session's tab out of its automation window into a
  background slot of the user's.
- createAutomationTarget reuses the session group's window only while every
  tab in it is the group's, and makes the new tab the active one there:
  upstream created it inactive, and Chrome drops input to a background tab.
- Input on a hidden page (window minimized or covered, tab in the
  background), read by page script: click and fill take their DOM forms at
  once, type focuses by script and inserts through Input.insertText, key
  dispatches page events (Enter in a form field submits it unless
  prevented), scroll scrolls the element or page; hover, drag and tap refuse
  with a reason. Chrome stalls mouse moves, wheel and touch on a page that
  renders no frames, and acknowledges then drops presses and keys on one
  that never rendered. Results say input: "dom".
- On a visible page whose frames are slow (a throttled window), a mouse path,
  drag or wheel flick that waits more than 100 ms for one event finishes in
  one more event instead of paying a frame per step.
- Model-facing error text names this cell's verbs ((chrome:snapshot),
  (chrome:tabs), :target/:url-includes/:title-includes) instead of pi's
  chrome_* tools.

Wire names (x-pi-chrome-version, the actions, the params) are upstream's. The
pi host side (extensions/chrome-profile-bridge/index.ts) is not vendored;
tools/nodecode-chrome/*.lisp re-implements that bridge for the Lisp organism. The
extension hard-codes http://127.0.0.1:17318 and reloads itself when the host
advertises a newer version than manifest.json, so the Lisp host advertises
exactly the version read from this folder's manifest.json, and START copies
this folder to ~/.nodecode/chrome-extension/, the one the operator loads: a
reload re-reads the folder an unpacked extension was loaded from, and this
one moves with every release.

To update: copy manifest.json, service_worker.js and snapshot_injected.js from
upstream, re-apply the changes above, bump the commit here and the fourth
version part, and re-run just chrome-check (the cell test pins the manifest
version).
